<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Which is the best summary of the proof in the example above, that <m>n^2 - 1</m> is divisible by 8 for every odd number <m>n</m>?</p>
<div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Check that <m>n^2 - 1</m> is a multiple of 8 for <m>n = 1, 3, 5, 7</m> and <m>9</m>, and conclude that it is a multiple of 8 for every odd number <m>n</m>.</li>
<li>Write <m>n = 2k + 1</m>, so that <m>n^2 - 1 = 4k(k + 1)</m>. One of the consecutive integers <m>k</m> and <m>k + 1</m> is even, so <m>n^2 - 1</m> is 8 times an integer.</li>
<li>Write <m>n = 2k + 1</m>, so that <m>n^2 - 1 = 4k^2 + 4k</m>. This is a multiple of 4, and so it is also a multiple of 8 for every odd <m>n</m>.</li>
<li>Since <m>n</m> is odd, <m>n^2</m> is odd, so <m>n^2 - 1</m> is even. Every even number is a multiple of 8, so <m>n^2 - 1</m> is a multiple of 8.</li>
</ol> </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A good summary names the structure and the key step: write the odd number as <m>2k + 1</m>, factorise <m>n^2 - 1</m> as <m>4k(k + 1)</m>, and use the fact that one of two consecutive integers is even to get a second factor 2. That is the second option.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Checking five values is the rough work, not the proof: it cannot cover every odd number.</p></li>
<li><p>A multiple of 4 need not be a multiple of 8: 12 is not. The proof needs the extra factor 2 from <m>k(k + 1)</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>The parity argument was the first attempt in the rough work, and it was abandoned: an even number need not be a multiple of 8 (2, 4, 6, 10, …).</p></li>
</ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>